<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4026.18">
  <POU Name="FB_LogCsvWriter" Id="{b85008c5-84dc-5f29-a8ef-2a5df33db034}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   Drains GVL_Log's 256-entry ring to a CSV file on the panel's Compact Flash.
   Phase 2 of docs/plans/log-csv-file.md. Called last in MAIN, after
   fbPersistentSave().

   WHY: the panel log is RAM only. Every entry is lost on power cycle, reset or
   download, so a fault overnight with nobody attached leaves nothing to read --
   which is exactly when the log matters most. This is the consumer the ring was
   designed for; its own comment has documented the drain contract
   (slot = nWriteIdx MOD 256) since long before anything drained it.

   Off by default. Nothing here touches the card until an operator sets
   GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE PLAN: no byte buffer.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The plan says to accumulate rows into an ARRAY[0..2047] OF BYTE and flush it
   with one FB_FileWrite. This writes rows individually with FB_FilePuts inside a
   single open/close instead, for three reasons that only became clear once the
   Phase 0 probe had run on the real device:

     1. THE FLASH COST UNIT IS THE OPEN->CLOSE CYCLE, not the individual write.
        Data written between an open and a close reaches the OS cache and is
        committed once. So N Puts inside one open/close costs the card the same
        as one Write of N rows -- the flash economy the byte buffer existed for is
        preserved without the buffer.
     2. FB_FilePuts + FOPEN_MODETEXT IS THE ONLY FORM VERIFIED ON THIS TARGET.
        Measured 2026-08-10 by retrieving the probe's file over FTP: a single $N
        arrives as CRLF, exactly RFC 4180, and FilePuts adds no ending of its
        own. Whether text-mode translation also applies to FB_FileWrite's raw
        buffer is unverified -- and if it does, cbWriteLen no longer equals the
        bytes on disk and the size accounting silently drifts.
     3. THE RING IS ALREADY THE BUFFER. 256 entries of it. A second buffer would
        duplicate it and add a second place for entries to be lost.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   LINE ENDINGS: EVERY ROW ENDS WITH A BARE $N. NEVER $R$N.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   FOPEN_MODETEXT already turns LF into CRLF. Writing $R$N would have it
   translate the $N again, giving \r\r\n and a corrupt file. This was very nearly
   "fixed" the wrong way on the strength of a miscounted byte total; the measured
   file settled it. Do not change it without re-measuring.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   NEVER FALL BACK TO \Temp\ OR THE DEVICE ROOT \.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Both are writable on this panel and BOTH LOSE THEIR CONTENTS ON RESTART -- the
   WinCE RAM object store. Proven 2026-08-10: files written there by the Phase 0
   probe were gone after an Activate, while the two on Compact Flash survived.
   A fallback to either would silently convert durable logging into RAM logging,
   reintroducing the exact defect this FB exists to fix, while every status field
   still reported healthy. A directory that cannot be opened must produce
   eState := FAILED with the error latched and visible. That is the whole point.
*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_LogCsvWriter
VAR CONSTANT
    RING        : UDINT := 256;         // GVL_Log.aLog size -- the drain window
    HIGH_WATER  : UDINT := 200;         // flush early rather than risk overflow
    FILE_TO     : TIME  := T#5S;
    RETRY_TO    : TIME  := T#30S;       // backoff after a failure
    MAX_SKIP    : INT   := 32;          // DBG entries discarded per scan, bounded
    HDR         : STRING(40) := 'time,severity,source,message';
    E_NOTFOUND  : UDINT := 1804;        // ADS "file not found", verified on the panel
END_VAR
VAR
    eStep       : INT;

    fbMkDir     : Tc2_System.FB_CreateDir;
    fbProbe     : Tc2_System.FB_FileOpen;   // existence test, separate instance
    fbOpen      : Tc2_System.FB_FileOpen;
    fbPuts      : Tc2_System.FB_FilePuts;
    fbClose     : Tc2_System.FB_FileClose;
    hFile       : UINT;

    // Our own read cursor into the ring. The ONLY state that must not be lost
    // mid-session, and deliberately not persistent: after a reboot the ring is
    // empty and nWriteIdx restarts at 0, so a restored cursor would be ahead of
    // the writer and would skip everything until it caught up.
    udiLastRead : UDINT;
    bCursorSet  : BOOL;

    udiPending  : UDINT;
    sFileName   : STRING(40);
    sLastFile   : STRING(40);
    sPath       : STRING(160);
    sDirNoSlash : STRING(80);
    sRow        : STRING(255);
    nSlot       : INT;
    nSkipped    : INT;

    bNeedHeader : BOOL;
    bLossRow    : BOOL;         // a synthetic "entries lost" row is owed
    udiLostNow  : UDINT;
    bDirTried   : BOOL;
    bClosing    : BOOL;         // draining because bEnabled went FALSE
    bErrLogged  : BOOL;         // edge guard -- one ERR in, one INFO out

    tonFlush    : TON;
    tonRetry    : TON;
    fbTrigOff   : F_TRIG;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 0 -- cursor baseline, and the enable/disable edges.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //

// First time we ever run, adopt whatever the ring can still supply rather than
// starting at nWriteIdx. Someone who enables logging after noticing trouble
// wants the entries that are still there, not only the ones from now on. Bounded
// by RING because older slots have already been overwritten.
IF NOT bCursorSet THEN
    bCursorSet := TRUE;
    IF GVL_Log.nWriteIdx > RING THEN
        udiLastRead := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - RING;
    ELSE
        udiLastRead := 0;
    END_IF
END_IF

// Turning logging OFF must not abandon a part-written flush or a full buffer:
// drain what is pending and close cleanly, THEN go idle.
fbTrigOff(CLK := NOT GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled);
IF fbTrigOff.Q AND (GVL_Log.nWriteIdx > udiLastRead) THEN
    bClosing := TRUE;
END_IF

udiPending := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - udiLastRead;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 1 -- flush trigger.
//
// The quiet timer is the flash-wear control: entries collect in the ring and
// reach the card once per uiFlushSec, so a busy second costs one open/close
// rather than dozens. HIGH_WATER is the safety valve -- at 200 of 256 pending
// we stop waiting for the timer, because passing 256 loses entries.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
tonFlush(IN  := (udiPending > 0) AND GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled,
         PT  := UDINT_TO_TIME(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.uiFlushSec * 1000));

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 2 -- the state machine.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
CASE eStep OF

// ---------------- 0: idle ---------------- //
0:
    // OFF means "not logging". BUFFER means "logging, entries accumulating in the
    // ring, waiting for the flush interval". Reporting OFF while enabled would
    // read as a fault on the Logs page when nothing is wrong.
    IF GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled THEN
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.BUFFER;
    ELSE
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.OFF;
    END_IF

    IF (GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.bEnabled OR bClosing)
       AND (udiPending > 0)
       AND (tonFlush.Q OR (udiPending >= HIGH_WATER) OR bClosing) THEN

        // OVERFLOW CHECK, before anything else touches the cursor. If more than
        // RING entries arrived since we last read, the oldest are already
        // overwritten and are gone -- unavoidable, the ring is finite. What is
        // NOT acceptable is losing them silently, so the count is published AND
        // a synthetic row goes into the file itself. A reader of the CSV must be
        // able to see the gap without also having to read a PLC symbol.
        IF udiPending > RING THEN
            udiLostNow := udiPending - RING;
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.udiEntriesDropped :=
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.udiEntriesDropped + udiLostNow;
            udiLastRead := GVL_Log.nWriteIdx - RING;
            bLossRow    := TRUE;
        END_IF

        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.BUFFER;
        eStep := 5;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 5: make sure the directory exists ---------------- //
// Once per session. Errors are IGNORED on purpose: "already exists" is an error
// from this FB and is also the normal case, and distinguishing it from a real
// failure by error number would be guessing. If the directory genuinely cannot
// be made, the open in state 20 fails and THAT is reported -- one authoritative
// error rather than two competing ones.
5:
    IF bDirTried THEN
        eStep := 10;
    ELSE
        sDirNoSlash := GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir;
        IF (LEN(sDirNoSlash) > 1)
           AND (RIGHT(sDirNoSlash, 1) = '\') THEN
            sDirNoSlash := LEFT(sDirNoSlash, LEN(sDirNoSlash) - 1);
        END_IF
        fbMkDir(sPathName := sDirNoSlash,
                ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
                bExecute  := TRUE,
                tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
        IF NOT fbMkDir.bBusy THEN
            fbMkDir(bExecute := FALSE);
            bDirTried := TRUE;
            eStep := 10;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 10: filename, and does it already exist? ---------------- //
10:
    IF GVL_Log.sToday = '0000-00-00' THEN
        // The panel RTC is unset. Naming a file from that would produce
        // something like flower-0000-00-00.csv, which sorts before every real
        // file and would be deleted first by the Phase 3 retention sweep -- so
        // the one file written while the clock was broken would be the first to
        // go. A distinct name keeps it identifiable and out of the date order.
        sFileName := 'flower-nodate.csv';
    ELSE
        sFileName := CONCAT(CONCAT('flower-', GVL_Log.sToday), '.csv');
    END_IF
    sPath := CONCAT(GVL_HmiPersistent.stLogFileCfg.sDir, sFileName);

    IF sFileName = sLastFile THEN
        bNeedHeader := FALSE;       // same file we appended to last time
        eStep := 20;
    ELSE
        eStep := 11;                // new name -- has to be tested
    END_IF

// ---------------- 11/12: existence probe ---------------- //
// A header belongs at the top of a file, once. Opening in append mode cannot
// tell us whether we created it, so we test first with a READ open: 1804 means
// absent, which is the one error code this project has verified on the panel.
// Costs one extra call per new file, i.e. once a day.
11:
    fbProbe(sPathName := sPath,
            nMode     := Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODEREAD,
            ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
            bExecute  := TRUE,
            tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbProbe.bBusy THEN
        fbProbe(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbProbe.bError THEN
            // Absent -> new file, needs a header. Any OTHER error is left for
            // the append open in state 20 to report, so there is one place that
            // decides the directory is unusable.
            bNeedHeader := (fbProbe.nErrId = E_NOTFOUND);
            eStep := 20;
        ELSE
            bNeedHeader := FALSE;
            hFile := fbProbe.hFile;
            eStep := 12;            // must close the probe handle
        END_IF
    END_IF

12:
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        hFile := 0;
        eStep := 20;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 20: open for append ---------------- //
20:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.OPENING;
    fbOpen(sPathName := sPath,
           nMode     := Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODEAPPEND OR Tc2_System.FOPEN_MODETEXT,
           ePath     := Tc2_System.PATH_GENERIC,
           bExecute  := TRUE,
           tTimeout  := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbOpen.bBusy THEN
        fbOpen(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbOpen.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbOpen.nErrId, sWhy := 'open failed');
        ELSE
            hFile := fbOpen.hFile;
            IF bNeedHeader THEN
                sLastFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sCurrentFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile := 0;
                eStep := 25;
            ELSE
                sLastFile := sFileName;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sCurrentFile := sFileName;
                eStep := 30;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 25: header row, new files only ---------------- //
25:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
    fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := CONCAT(HDR, '$N'),
           bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
        fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbPuts.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'header write failed');
        ELSE
            // +1 because text mode expands the single $N to CRLF on disk.
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(HDR)) + 2;
            bNeedHeader := FALSE;
            eStep := 30;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 30: the synthetic loss row, if one is owed ---------------- //
30:
    IF NOT bLossRow THEN
        eStep := 31;
    ELSE
        GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
        sRow := CONCAT(CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.sNow), ',"WARN","LogCsv","'),
                CONCAT(UDINT_TO_STRING(udiLostNow),
                       ' entries lost - CSV writer fell behind the ring"$N'));
        fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := sRow,
               bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
        IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
            fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
            IF fbPuts.bError THEN
                Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'loss row write failed');
            ELSE
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                    GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(sRow)) + 1;
                bLossRow := FALSE;
                eStep := 31;
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 31: one row per pass until caught up ---------------- //
31:
    IF udiLastRead >= GVL_Log.nWriteIdx THEN
        eStep := 40;
    ELSE
        // Discard DBG without a file call. Unconditional -- independent of
        // GVL_Log.bDebugMode, which still governs the on-screen ring. DBG is the
        // 1 Hz robot keep-alive trace; it would bury the file and burn the card.
        // Bounded per scan so a long DBG run cannot stretch the PlcTask.
        nSkipped := 0;
        WHILE (udiLastRead < GVL_Log.nWriteIdx)
              AND (nSkipped < MAX_SKIP) DO
            nSlot := UDINT_TO_INT(udiLastRead MOD RING);
            IF GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].eSev = E_LogSev.DBG THEN
                udiLastRead := udiLastRead + 1;
                nSkipped := nSkipped + 1;
            ELSE
                EXIT;
            END_IF
        END_WHILE

        IF udiLastRead >= GVL_Log.nWriteIdx THEN
            eStep := 40;
        ELSE
            GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.WRITING;
            nSlot := UDINT_TO_INT(udiLastRead MOD RING);
            sRow  := RowFor(nSlot := nSlot);
            fbPuts(hFile := hFile, sLine := sRow,
                   bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
            IF NOT fbPuts.bBusy THEN
                fbPuts(bExecute := FALSE);
                IF fbPuts.bError THEN
                    Fail(nErr := fbPuts.nErrId, sWhy := 'row write failed');
                ELSE
                    // Cursor advances ONLY after the write is confirmed. If it
                    // advanced first, a failure here would drop the entry
                    // permanently and invisibly -- the one outcome this whole FB
                    // is arranged to prevent.
                    udiLastRead := udiLastRead + 1;
                    GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile :=
                        GVL_Log.stLogFile.uiBytesInFile + INT_TO_UDINT(LEN(sRow)) + 1;
                END_IF
            END_IF
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 40: close ---------------- //
40:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.CLOSING;
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        IF fbClose.bError THEN
            Fail(nErr := fbClose.nErrId, sWhy := 'close failed');
        ELSE
            hFile := 0;
            // One INFO out, matching the one ERR in. Same discipline as
            // FB_RobotTcpClient's bStateUnanswered: a repeating condition must
            // not be level-logged into a 20-entry ring.
            IF bErrLogged THEN
                bErrLogged := FALSE;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.iErrorCode := 0;
                GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText := '';
                F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.INFO, sSource := 'LogCsv',
                           sMsg  := CONCAT('CSV log recovered, writing ',
                                           sFileName));
            END_IF
            tonFlush(IN := FALSE);      // restart the quiet interval
            bClosing := FALSE;
            eStep := 0;
        END_IF
    END_IF

// ---------------- 90: release the handle on the way to FAILED ---------------- //
// Reached only from Fail() and only when a handle is still open. The close result
// is IGNORED: we are already reporting the original error, and replacing it with
// a close error would lose the diagnosis that matters. What we need from here is
// the handle back, not a second opinion.
90:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.CLOSING;
    fbClose(hFile := hFile, bExecute := TRUE, tTimeout := FILE_TO);
    IF NOT fbClose.bBusy THEN
        fbClose(bExecute := FALSE);
        hFile := 0;
        eStep := 99;
    END_IF

// ---------------- 99: failed -- back off, then retry ---------------- //
// Retry on a TIMER, never every scan. A bad sDir or a full card would otherwise
// produce a file call per scan for as long as the machine runs.
99:
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState := E_LogFileState.FAILED;
    tonRetry(IN := TRUE, PT := RETRY_TO);
    IF tonRetry.Q THEN
        tonRetry(IN := FALSE);
        bDirTried := FALSE;             // maybe the card came back
        sLastFile := '';                // re-test existence
        eStep := 0;
    END_IF

END_CASE

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
// Section 3 -- the STRING mirror of eState.
//
// Required, not decoration: the classic VISU cannot stringify an enum, so a text
// output bound to eState renders the raw number. Every enum in this project
// carries such a mirror for that reason.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
CASE GVL_Log.stLogFile.eState OF
    E_LogFileState.OFF:      GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'OFF';
    E_LogFileState.BUFFER:   GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'BUFFER';
    E_LogFileState.OPENING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'OPENING';
    E_LogFileState.WRITING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'WRITING';
    E_LogFileState.CLOSING:  GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'CLOSING';
    E_LogFileState.ROTATING: GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'ROTATING';
    E_LogFileState.FAILED:   GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := 'FAILED';
ELSE
    GVL_Log.stLogFile.sStateText := '?';
END_CASE
]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="Csv" Id="{1b4287c1-9ff1-538d-8e24-2268967534d3}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   RFC 4180 quote one field: wrap in double quotes, and double any quote inside.

   NOT theoretical. Robot frames reach log messages as 'SYNC:NAME=VALUE,...' and
   message text already carries commas and parentheses, so an unquoted field
   would split a row into the wrong number of columns and silently corrupt every
   downstream reader. Verified end-to-end on the panel 2026-08-10: a message
   containing both a comma and a doubled quote came back off the card via FTP and
   parsed as one intact field.

   Every field is quoted, including the fixed ones. Uniform rows are easier to
   reason about than rows where quoting depends on content, and the cost is two
   bytes.

   THE PARAMETER IS *NOT* CALLED sIn. IEC identifiers are case-insensitive, so
   `sIn` IS `SIN`, the standard trigonometric function -- and the whole POU then
   fails to parse with "'SIN' needs exactly '1' Operands" at every call site. Same
   class of trap as the Logs page that could not be named `Log`. The reserved set
   includes the trig family (SIN COS TAN ASIN ACOS ATAN) alongside the arithmetic
   and string functions CLAUDE.md already lists.
*)
METHOD Csv : STRING(200)
VAR_INPUT
    sField : STRING(80);
END_VAR
VAR
    j    : INT;
    n    : INT;
    sOut : STRING(200);
    c    : STRING(1);
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[sOut := '"';
n := LEN(sField);

FOR j := 1 TO n DO
    c := MID(sField, 1, j);            // MID(str, len, pos) -- one char at j
    IF c = '"' THEN
        sOut := CONCAT(sOut, '""');
    ELSE
        sOut := CONCAT(sOut, c);
    END_IF
END_FOR

Csv := CONCAT(sOut, '"');
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="RowFor" Id="{ae6940c9-79c5-559c-a634-3cc17c6a8310}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   Build one CSV row from a ring slot, ending in a bare $N.

   Column order matches the HDR constant. Worst case fits: the four source
   fields are STRING(8), STRING(8), STRING(20) and STRING(80), so fully
   quote-doubled they come to 18 + 18 + 42 + 162, plus three commas and the
   newline = 244 of the 255 available.

   $N and NOT $R$N -- FOPEN_MODETEXT already produces CRLF, and doubling it
   gives \r\r\n. See the FB header.
*)
METHOD RowFor : STRING(255)
VAR_INPUT
    nSlot : INT;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[RowFor := CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sTime), ',');
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sSevText), ','));
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sSource), ','));
RowFor := CONCAT(RowFor, CONCAT(Csv(sField := GVL_Log.aLog[nSlot].sMsg), '$N'));
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="Fail" Id="{e43e6ecc-ad8d-5b9f-81d5-9c5aa2afc35b}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
   One place that gives up on a flush: latch the error, release the handle, log
   once, and drop into the backoff state.

   THE LOG IS EDGE-GUARDED, and that is the point of centralising this. A full
   card or a bad sDir repeats every retry interval, and level-logging it would
   flush the 20-entry aRecent ring and hide whatever caused the trouble --
   exactly the defect this project already has open against
   FB_RobotTcpClient's connect-failure path. One ERR in, one INFO out on
   recovery (state 40).

   IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE HANDLE ITSELF. An earlier version did, with one
   bExecute TRUE/FALSE pair in the same scan, which starts the close and disarms
   it before it can finish -- so the handle leaks. Harmless once; but a failure
   that repeats every RETRY_TO leaks one every 30 s for as long as the machine
   runs, and exhausting the runtime's handle table would then present as the file
   blocks having stopped working. So a still-open handle routes through state 90,
   which closes it asynchronously and properly before the backoff.
*)
METHOD Fail : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
    nErr : UDINT;
    sWhy : STRING(40);
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[GVL_Log.stLogFile.iErrorCode := nErr;
GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText :=
    CONCAT(CONCAT(sWhy, ' err '), UDINT_TO_STRING(nErr));

IF NOT bErrLogged THEN
    bErrLogged := TRUE;
    F_LogEvent(eSev := E_LogSev.ERR, sSource := 'LogCsv',
               sMsg  := CONCAT(CONCAT(GVL_Log.stLogFile.sErrorText, ' ('),
                               CONCAT(sPath, ')')));
END_IF

bClosing := FALSE;

// A handle we opened is still open -- close it properly first (state 90) rather
// than abandoning it. See the header: abandoning leaks one handle per retry.
IF hFile <> 0 THEN
    eStep := 90;
ELSE
    eStep := 99;
END_IF

Fail := TRUE;
]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>
